The rapid advancement of information and communication technologies has fundamentally reshaped educational systems, making digital competence a critical requirement for educational leaders. This systematic literature review examines the ICT competencies necessary for educational managers to effectively lead technology integration in schools and higher education institutions. As key decision-makers, these leaders must not only understand digital tools but also drive innovation, support staff development, and align technological initiatives with pedagogical goals. The study synthesizes findings from recent research to identify core competencies, analyze barriers to adoption, and highlight effective professional development strategies.

A central finding of the review is that effective educational leadership in the digital age requires a combination of technical proficiency, information literacy, pedagogical knowledge, and change management skills. Technical proficiency enables managers to operate learning management systems, data analytics platforms, and communication tools essential for modern administration. Information literacy allows them to evaluate digital resources critically, interpret performance data, and make evidence-based decisions. Perhaps most importantly, pedagogical knowledge equips leaders to guide teachers in using technology to enhance student engagement and support personalized learning. Additionally, strong leadership and change management abilities are crucial for overcoming resistance, fostering a culture of innovation, and ensuring ethical use of technology, including issues of privacy, cybersecurity, and digital equity.

Despite the recognized importance of these competencies, the review identifies several persistent barriers to their development and application. A major challenge is the lack of targeted professional development opportunities tailored to the specific needs of educational managers. Many existing training programs focus on classroom teachers rather than administrators, leaving leaders underprepared to manage digital transformation. Resistance to change within institutional cultures also hinders progress, particularly when stakeholders do not see the value of new technologies. Furthermore, resource constraints—such as limited budgets, inadequate infrastructure, and insufficient technical support—often prevent effective implementation. The absence of a clear strategic vision for technology integration exacerbates these challenges, leading to fragmented and inconsistent adoption across departments.

To address these barriers, the review highlights several effective training practices. Tailored professional development programs that begin with a needs assessment are more likely to meet the real-world demands faced by educational leaders. Blended learning models, combining online modules with face-to-face workshops, offer flexibility and accommodate busy schedules while catering to diverse learning preferences. Equally important is the provision of ongoing support, including mentorship, peer collaboration, and follow-up sessions, which help sustain learning over time. Training that incorporates real-world scenarios, case studies, and hands-on activities prepares managers to apply their skills in practical contexts, increasing confidence and competence.

In conclusion, this research underscores that successful technology integration in education depends not just on access to tools, but on the leadership capacity to use them effectively. Educational managers must be equipped with a well-rounded set of ICT competencies to navigate the complexities of the digital era. By investing in context-sensitive, continuous professional development and addressing structural and cultural barriers, institutions can empower their leaders to drive meaningful change. The implications extend beyond administrative efficiency—effective digital leadership enhances teaching quality, improves student outcomes, and ensures that education systems remain relevant in a rapidly evolving technological landscape. This study provides a valuable framework for policymakers, training providers, and academic leaders seeking to strengthen the digital readiness of educational management.